  "content": "<p>Check your understanding of this lesson. Your answers aren't recorded.</p>\n<multiple-choice question=\"Why add support (holding) edges near a hard edge before subdividing?\" single-option=\"\" randomize=\"\">\n  <input type=\"checkbox\" value=\"To keep the edge sharp when the mesh is smoothed\" data-correct=\"true\">\n  <input type=\"checkbox\" value=\"To reduce the polygon count\">\n  <input type=\"checkbox\" value=\"To create UV seams\">\n  <input type=\"checkbox\" value=\"To make the object symmetrical\">\n</multiple-choice>\n<true-false-question question=\"Modifiers such as Bevel, Mirror and Boolean let you change a model without permanently altering its base mesh.\">\n  <input type=\"checkbox\" value=\"True\" data-correct=\"true\">\n  <input type=\"checkbox\" value=\"False\">\n</true-false-question>\n<self-check title=\"Think it through\">\n  <p slot=\"question\">When is a boolean operation a good choice, and what problems should you watch for?</p>\n  <p>Booleans are quick for cutting holes and combining shapes, especially while blocking out. Watch for messy n-gons, shading artifacts and non-manifold geometry, and clean up the topology or keep the boolean as a non-destructive modifier.</p>\n</self-check>\n"
}